--Description / Thinking process:
--1. In the earlier preview we saw that every column in GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US.LOGS
--   is stored in lowercase; Snowflake treats quoted identifiers as case-sensitive, so we must reference them
--   exactly as `"block_number"`, `"data"`, and `"block_timestamp"`.
--2. The task is to measure block “size”.  A simple proxy is the byte-length of the hexadecimal `data`
--   payload emitted by all logs in a block.  `LENGTH("data")` returns that length.
--3. We need blocks occurring on or after 2023-01-01.  Timestamps in this table are in microseconds,
--   so 2023-01-01 00:00:00 UTC = 1 672 531 200 000 000 µs.
--4. Aggregate with SUM over each `"block_number"`, producing `"block_data_bytes"`.
--5. Order by the sum descending and limit to the top 20 to inspect the largest blocks.
SELECT
    "block_number",
    SUM(LENGTH("data")) AS "block_data_bytes"
FROM "GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN"."GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US"."LOGS"
WHERE "block_timestamp" >= 1672531200000000     -- 2023-01-01 in µs
GROUP BY "block_number"
ORDER BY "block_data_bytes" DESC
LIMIT 20;
